Deferred: 16-byte cons cells via a separate paged cons heap

Status: deferred (idea captured during the Slice "compact cons / Option A" work).

Context
-------
A cons currently costs 48 bytes in two heap blocks (Object{header,class,slots}
24B + Cons{header,car,cdr} 24B).  "Option A" (done) inlines car/cdr into a
single 32-byte block {header, class=CONS_Class, car, cdr}, objcount=3 — one
allocation, one dereference, ~33% less memory.  That keeps the uniform
mark-sweep heap and per-block header, so it is low risk.

Further improvement (this issue)
--------------------------------
Drive cons cells down to the theoretical minimum of 16 bytes (just car+cdr)
WITHOUT tagged pointers, by giving conses their OWN heap:

  - Allocate cons cells from a dedicated set of pages (an array/arena) separate
    from the general object heap.  Each slot is exactly {Ref car; Ref cdr} = 16B.
  - The size, objcount(=2 references) and class(=CONS_Class) are IDENTICAL for
    every cell, so they are stored ONCE for the whole arena (or implied by the
    arena), not per cell.
  - class_of / consp / the GC recognise a cons by checking whether the pointer
    falls inside the cons arena's address range (a range check), NOT by reading
    a per-object header — so it stays portable and safe (unlike low-bit tagged
    pointers, which depend on alignment / compiler / target assumptions).

Cost / why deferred
-------------------
  - Two heaps now: the cons arena needs its own allocation (free list of 16B
    cells) and its own mark/sweep participation, plus dynamic growth (add pages)
    coordinated with the main heap.  More moving parts.
  - mark() must, for any Ref, decide "is this in the cons arena?" cheaply (sorted
    page table / range test) and then treat its two words as car/cdr.
  - sweep()/valid_block_start_p / next_block / coalescing are header-based today;
    the cons arena needs parallel, header-free logic.

Payoff: 48B -> 16B per cons (3x), fewer GC objects, better locality — best done
after Option A is stable and if memory/throughput still warrants it (the ANSI
suite's multi-GB working set is dominated by cons cells, so this is the biggest
remaining single memory lever).

Safety note: prefer this paged-arena approach over tagged-pointer fixnums/conses
— it does not rely on pointer-bit availability and works across C compilers and
target machines.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
AUDIT 2026-06-20 (v1.1.3) — VERDICT: INTENTIONALLY DEFERRED (no change)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
"Option A" (the 32-byte single-block inlined cons) IS done (kernel_types.h:105,
kernel.c:1154-1192). The separate paged 16-byte cons heap (this issue's subject)
is NOT implemented anywhere — no arena/paged/header-free code exists. Correctly
deferred ("if memory/throughput still warrants it"). Keep open.
